<h2>The case and the custody clock</h2>
<p>The incident dates to 25 May 2020: a dispute in New Mustafabad in which the deceased, Danish, suffered stab wounds attributed to the applicant and a head injury from a brick attributed to a co-accused. Danish died on 7 June 2020. The applicant was arrested on 13 June 2020. Charges were framed only on 7 December 2022 — under Sections 302, 326, 324, 34 IPC and Section 25 of the Arms Act — and by late 2026 the prosecution&rsquo;s witnesses had still not been exhausted. As the Court recorded, <em>&ldquo;the trial is, thus, unlikely to conclude in the near future.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>The reasoning</h2>
<p>The order rests on the settled trilogy of bail considerations in long-custody cases. First, the length of incarceration: over six years as an undertrial, a period that the pace of the trial gave no prospect of ending soon. Second, the state of the evidence: all material witnesses had been examined, so the classic apprehension — that an accused on bail will influence the witnesses who matter — had lost its force. Third, conduct: jail conduct was satisfactory, and the applicant had been released on interim bail thrice in 2022 without misconduct, a track record courts treat as a practical test run for regular bail.</p>
<p>Against these, the State&rsquo;s strongest card was the gravity of a Section 302 charge. The Court answered with the principle that governs: <em>&ldquo;The gravity of the offence, by itself, is no ground to refuse bail.&rdquo;</em> Gravity informs the assessment of flight risk and tampering; it is not an independent sentence served in advance.</p>
<p>One further observation, expressly confined to the bail stage, concerned the medical record: the post-mortem traced death to shock caused by a blunt-force head injury, while the stab injuries were the ones attributed to the applicant. The Court flagged the mismatch for what it was worth at this stage, taking care to record no view on the merits.</p>

<h2>How long-custody bail arguments are built</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody arithmetic.</strong> Date of arrest, date of charge, witnesses examined against witnesses cited — the numbers, laid out plainly, carry the application further than adjectives.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Witness status.</strong> Once material witnesses are examined, the tampering objection weakens decisively. Orders summarising the deposition stage are essential annexures.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Conduct record.</strong> Prior interim bail availed without breach, and a clean jail record (nominal roll), convert an abstract promise of good behaviour into demonstrated fact.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Article 21 framing.</strong> Prolonged pre-trial detention engages the right to a speedy trial; the longer the custody and the slower the trial, the heavier the State&rsquo;s burden to justify continued detention.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">Bail in serious offences remains discretionary and fact-specific; conditions are tailored to the record, and findings at the bail stage decide nothing about guilt. Under the current procedural code, regular bail applications of this kind are moved under Section 483 of the BNSS, 2023 — the successor to Section 439 CrPC.</div>
